{% doc %}
  A sneak peek at the NEXT Yoink, for the shopper who has decided against
  today's.

  WHY IT SITS BELOW THE ADD-TO-CART AND NOT BESIDE THE PRICE. This is the only
  element on the page whose job is to be read by someone who is not going to
  buy. Between the price and the button it would interrupt the highest-intent
  moment on the site with a reason to leave; underneath, it is only reached by
  someone who has already seen the price, seen the button, and kept scrolling.
  The conditional opening line does the same work in words — it addresses the
  non-buyer explicitly, so a buyer skims past it.

  WHAT IT MAY NEVER SAY.

  Not the product, and not its image. Collective SKUs are not exclusive, so
  naming tomorrow's item gives a deal-hunter a day to find it cheaper somewhere
  else — the same reasoning that keeps the vendor out of every social post.

  Not the discount. `discount_percent` on a queued deal is INTENT: activation
  clamps the price to cost and reports the discount actually applied, so a
  queued 45 can land at 42. Quoting it here would be a promise the pricing path
  is explicitly designed not to keep.

  What is left is true without being identifying: the category, the price band,
  and the drop time. All three are read from Shopify. The band and category come
  from the product's own `band-` and `cat-` tags, which the catalogue agent
  maintains, so they cannot drift from what the product actually is.

  Renders NOTHING when there is no next deal, which is the honest answer on a
  day the queue has run out — and is why the runway invariant exists.

  Today's deal needs no excluding: the search takes the earliest window that has
  not STARTED yet, and a live deal started in the past by definition.

  @param {string} [heading] - Opening line. Phrase it as a question to the
    non-buyer, so a buyer skims past it.
  @param {boolean} [show_signup] - Offer the email capture underneath.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign now = 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
  assign next_deal = null
  assign next_starts = 0
  assign live_ends = 0

  comment
    Earliest deal that has not started yet. Iterating rather than relying on
    metaobject order, which is not guaranteed to be chronological.
  endcomment
  for entry in shop.metaobjects.daily_deal.values
    assign starts = entry.starts_at.value | date: '%s' | plus: 0
    assign ends = entry.ends_at.value | date: '%s' | plus: 0

    comment
      An inverted window can never run, so it must never be teased. queueDeal
      refuses to create these now, but one already exists in the queue.
    endcomment
    if ends <= starts
      continue
    endif

    if starts <= now and ends > now
      assign live_ends = ends
    endif

    if starts > now
      if next_starts == 0 or starts < next_starts
        assign next_starts = starts
        assign next_deal = entry
      endif
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    Deals run back-to-back in 24-hour windows, so the next one almost always
    opens at the exact instant this one closes — which made a second countdown
    tick in perfect lockstep with the first, to the second, forever. Two clocks
    saying one thing is not urgency, it is noise, and it made the real one
    look decorative.

    So the timing is a sentence when the handover is seamless and a date when
    it is not. It is checked rather than assumed: a gap day would make "the
    moment this one ends" false, and a claim that is usually true is exactly
    the kind this store does not make.
  endcomment
  assign seamless = false
  if live_ends > 0 and next_starts == live_ends
    assign seamless = true
  endif
-%}

{%- if next_deal != blank and next_deal.product.value != blank -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign next_product = next_deal.product.value
    assign band_label = ''
    assign cat_label = ''

    for tag in next_product.tags
      comment
        A PRICE BAND ONLY HELPS WHEN IT IS LOW. "Under $10" is a reason to come
        back; "over $50" is a reason not to, and on a store whose whole promise
        is a bargain it reads as a warning. The pull is asymmetric, so the copy
        is too: the cheap bands are stated, the expensive ones are simply not
        mentioned and the category carries the line alone.

        This is omission, not misdirection — nothing here claims tomorrow is
        cheap. Same shape as the claimed bar, which shows a real number or
        nothing rather than dressing up a weak one.
      endcomment
      if band_label == '' and tag contains 'band-'
        assign band = tag | remove_first: 'band-'
        case band
          when 'under-10'
            assign band_label = 'under $10'
          when '10-20'
            assign band_label = 'under $20'
          when '20-30'
            assign band_label = 'under $30'
        endcase
      endif
    endfor

    comment
      The category collection carries a shopper-facing title, which is the one
      overridden away from Shopify's taxonomy wording — "Food & Beverages"
      rather than the segment name that still ends in Tobacco.
    endcomment
    for tag in next_product.tags
      if cat_label == '' and tag contains 'cat-'
        assign cat_collection = collections[tag]
        if cat_collection != blank and cat_collection.title != blank
          comment
            NOT downcased. The collection title is already the shopper-facing
            wording — it is the string that turns Shopify's "Food, Beverages &
            Tobacco" segment into "Food & Beverages" — and lowercasing it
            turned a proper category into "in media", which reads like a typo
            rather than a category.
          endcomment
          assign cat_label = cat_collection.title
        endif
      endif
    endfor
  -%}

  <aside class="aj-tomorrow" aria-labelledby="aj-tomorrow-heading">
    <p class="aj-tomorrow__heading" id="aj-tomorrow-heading">
      {{ heading | default: "Today's Yoink not your thing?" }}
    </p>

    {%- comment -%}
      THE TEASER LEADS WHEN THERE IS ONE. It is written by the enrichment agent
      under a rule that it may evoke a person or a moment and never the object,
      because these SKUs are not exclusive and naming tomorrow's item hands a
      deal-hunter a day to find it cheaper. It is also the only line here
      written for a shopper rather than a colleague.

      The category-and-band sentence stays underneath as the factual half, and
      carries the page on its own when the teaser is blank — which the prompt
      treats as a good answer rather than a failure.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign teaser = next_product.metafields.enrich.teaser.value -%}
    {%- if teaser != blank -%}
      <p class="aj-tomorrow__teaser">{{ teaser | escape }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}

    <p class="aj-tomorrow__body">
      {%- if cat_label != blank and band_label != blank -%}
        The next one is <strong>{{ cat_label }}</strong>, priced
        <strong>{{ band_label }}</strong>.
      {%- elsif cat_label != blank -%}
        The next one is <strong>{{ cat_label }}</strong>.
      {%- elsif band_label != blank -%}
        The next one is priced <strong>{{ band_label }}</strong>.
      {%- else -%}
        There's a new one on the way.
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- comment -%}
        The tags below deliberately do NOT use the whitespace-stripping hyphen
        form. Stripping ate the space between the two sentences and the page
        printed "over $50.It drops the moment this one ends."
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {% if seamless %}
        It drops the moment this one ends.
      {% else %}
        It drops {{ next_deal.starts_at.value | date: '%A %-d %B, %-l%P' }}.
      {% endif %}
    </p>

    {%- if show_signup -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The reason to hand over an email, rather than a bare "join our list".
        Capture lives only in the footer today, where nothing is offered for it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The form carries an `id`, which is what makes the page come BACK here.
        Shopify redirects to `?customer_posted=true` and jumps to the form's id
        as an anchor; without one the browser lands wherever it likes — which on
        a long deal page meant scrolling somewhere else entirely and looking as
        though the click did nothing at all.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- form 'customer', id: 'aj-tomorrow-signup', class: 'aj-tomorrow__form' -%}
        <input type="hidden" name="contact[tags]" value="yoink-preview">

        {%- if form.posted_successfully? -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            THE FORM IS REPLACED, not annotated. Leaving the input and button in
            place with a line of small text underneath is what made a successful
            signup read as a no-op: the thing you interacted with looked exactly
            as it did before you touched it. Removing it is the confirmation.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <p class="aj-tomorrow__success" role="status">
            <strong>You're on the list.</strong>
            Tomorrow's Yoink lands in your inbox before it goes live.
          </p>
        {%- else -%}
          <label class="aj-tomorrow__label" for="aj-tomorrow-email">
            Want to know what it is before anyone else?
          </label>
          <div class="aj-tomorrow__row">
            <input
              type="email"
              id="aj-tomorrow-email"
              name="contact[email]"
              class="aj-tomorrow__input"
              placeholder="you@example.com"
              autocomplete="email"
              required
            >
            {%- comment -%}
              NOT the theme's primary `button` class. That renders identically to
              Add to cart, so the page offered two equally weighted black buttons
              and the second-level message looked like a second offer. This is
              deliberately quieter than the CTA it sits beneath.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <button type="submit" class="aj-tomorrow__button">Send it to me</button>
          </div>
          {%- if form.errors -%}
            <p class="aj-tomorrow__note aj-tomorrow__note--error" role="alert">
              {{ form.errors.messages['email'] | default: 'That email did not look right.' }}
            </p>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endform -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </aside>
{%- endif -%}
